WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.514
Authorization answers the question, what
is this identity allowed to do.

2
00:00:07.594 --> 00:00:15.626
In Minimal APIs, authorization should be
explicit through endpoint or group-level

3
00:00:15.706 --> 00:00:16.498
policy.

4
00:00:16.578 --> 00:00:23.252
Private APIs should generally start from
deny-by-default thinking:

5
00:00:23.332 --> 00:00:28.762
an endpoint is not accessible just because
it has a route.

6
00:00:28.842 --> 00:00:36.082
It becomes accessible when the team
intentionally defines the access rule.

7
00:00:36.162 --> 00:00:44.421
RequireAuthorization is useful because it
makes access policy visible on an endpoint

8
00:00:44.501 --> 00:00:45.971
or route group.

9
00:00:46.051 --> 00:00:52.386
The exact syntax is less important than
the engineering practice:

10
00:00:52.466 --> 00:01:00.159
reviewers should see that the endpoint
requires authenticated access or a named

11
00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:05.895
policy, and they should understand what
that policy means.

12
00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:10.500
Named policies support reusable access
rules.

13
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:16.350
A policy can represent claims, roles,
tenant requirements,

14
00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:23.670
administrative access, service identity
expectations, or custom checks.

15
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:31.782
Named policies are easier to review than
repeated inline decisions because the rule

16
00:01:31.862 --> 00:01:36.387
has a central definition and a meaningful
name.

17
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:41.218
Role-based and claim-based checks can be
useful,

18
00:01:41.298 --> 00:01:45.823
but they should match real business
decisions.

19
00:01:45.903 --> 00:01:50.881
A role may be too broad for a record-level
operation.

20
00:01:50.961 --> 00:01:58.088
A claim may describe identity context but
not prove ownership of a specific

21
00:01:58.168 --> 00:01:59.186
resource.

22
00:01:59.266 --> 00:02:07.185
Resource-sensitive checks may require
loading the target resource and verifying

23
00:02:07.265 --> 00:02:14.505
ownership, tenant, state, or permission
before completing the operation.

24
00:02:14.585 --> 00:02:20.581
Group-level authorization helps protect
related endpoints,

25
00:02:20.661 --> 00:02:25.978
but sensitive endpoints still need
individual review.

26
00:02:26.058 --> 00:02:33.298
A route group can apply a common policy to
a tenant API or administrative API,

27
00:02:33.378 --> 00:02:36.772
but an export, deletion, approval,

28
00:02:36.852 --> 00:02:42.056
or configuration operation may require
extra checks.

29
00:02:42.136 --> 00:02:49.602
Shared policy should not become a reason
to skip endpoint-specific thinking.

30
00:02:49.682 --> 00:02:54.660
UI-only restrictions, route naming, hidden
links,

31
00:02:54.740 --> 00:03:01.301
or client-side checks are not server-side
authorization controls.

32
00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:04.662
They may improve user experience,

33
00:03:04.742 --> 00:03:10.511
but Minimal API endpoints must enforce
access on the server.

34
00:03:10.591 --> 00:03:18.284
Every protected operation should be safe
even if called directly by a legitimate

35
00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:24.020
client with a valid identity but
insufficient permission.
